+Subject: channelid
+
+Adapted: SSL_F/SSL_R codes that collide with 1.0.1u renumbered (GET_CHANNEL_ID
+317->319, CHANNEL_ID_SIGNATURE_INVALID 371->381, GOT_CHANNEL_ID_BEFORE_A_CCS
+372->382, NO_P256_SUPPORT 373->380 as AOSP 5.1, CHANNEL_ID_NOT_P256 375->383);
+the server CHANNEL_ID state sets SSL3_FLAGS_CCS_OK like upstream NEXT_PROTO
+(1.0.1u CCS fix); ssl3_get_channel_id sets SSL_ST_ERR on failure like 1.0.1u
+ssl3_get_next_proto; ssl3_send_channel_id declared outside the NPN guard.
+kktls hardening: the client accepts the Channel ID echo only if it offered the
+extension, and ssl3_send_channel_id fails cleanly without a key (KitKat
+dereferenced a NULL key on an unsolicited echo).
+
+kktls fixes (2026-10-09): ssl3_send_channel_id sets SSL_ST_ERR on every
+fatal error (the missing key included) and returns ssl3_do_write directly, as
+the upstream senders do; ssl3_get_channel_id frees its EVP_MD_CTX and sets
+SSL_ST_ERR when the handshake hash fails (leak inherited from KitKat);
+SSL_CTRL_SET_CHANNEL_ID sets tlsext_channel_id_enabled only after the P-256
+key is accepted (KitKat set it first, so a rejected key still offered the
+extension), and the SSL_CTX variant returns 0 for a non-P-256 key.
